Document (dev.stxt.website): Tools

	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Last modif: 2026-08-21

	Header: Tools

	Assert >>
		Everything on this page —playground, extension, command line and libraries—
		implements **the same five specifications** and reports **the same error
		codes**. Pick a tool for convenience, not for compatibility.

	Content >>
		@STXT@ needs no installation to get started. Depending on what you want to do:

		| I want to…                                     | Use…                                                       |
		|------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
		| Try the language right now, without installing | The [playground](https://play.stxt.dev)                    |
		| Write documents every day                      | The **VS Code** extension                                  |
		| Validate from a script, a `Makefile` or CI     | The `stxt` command line                                    |
		| Read or generate @STXT@ from my own program    | A library: **TypeScript**, **Java** or **Python**          |

	Subheader: Playground

	Content >>
		[play.stxt.dev](https://play.stxt.dev) is an editor in the browser, designed as a
		simplified VS Code: several documents at once, their schemas or templates next to
		them, syntax and validation errors as you type, grammar-driven autocompletion and
		hover with the definition of each node.

		* Nothing to install and no account to create; your work is kept in the browser
		  itself.
		* It comes seeded with example documents to play with and break.
		* A document can be shared with a link that carries the content inside.

		It is the place to follow the [tutorial](lang-tutorial) without leaving the browser.

	Subheader: VS Code extension

	Content >>
		The official extension is
		[STXT – Semantic Text (`stxt-lang.stxt`)](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=stxt-lang.stxt).
		From the extensions view, searching for `stxt`, or from the terminal:

	Listing >>
		code --install-extension stxt-lang.stxt

	Content >>
		What it brings:

		* Syntax errors in real time, and schema errors when a grammar applies.
		* Semantic highlighting driven by the parser itself.
		* Completion with the nodes the schema allows and the values of an `ENUM`.
		* Hover information: node, canonical name, type, values and description.
		* Go to definition (F12): from a node to the `Node:` of its schema, or to its
		  line inside the `Structure` of its template.
		* Document formatting.
		* Schema and template resolution from the `.stxt/` directories of the project,
		  the user and the system, as described in the [discovery reference](stxt-discovery-ref).

	Subheader: Command line

	Content >>
		The `stxt` command is the interface for scripts, `Makefile`s and continuous
		integration pipelines. It is published on npm as
		[`@stxt-lang/cli`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@stxt-lang/cli) and requires
		Node 20 or newer.

	Listing >>
		npm install -g @stxt-lang/cli
		stxt --version

	Content >>
		Or without installing anything permanently:

	Listing >>
		npx @stxt-lang/cli validate docs/

	Content >>
		The commands:

		| Command          | What it does                                                                  |
		|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
		| `stxt validate`  | Parses and validates one or more files or directories; silent when all pass   |
		| `stxt format`    | Reformats (`--tabs` / `--spaces`); `--check` for CI, `--write` to save        |
		| `stxt describe`  | Prints the logical tree of a document as JSON (STXT-TREE-SPEC)                |
		| `stxt schemas`   | Tells which schemas and templates apply in a directory, and from which level  |
		| `stxt install`   | Installs a definition into `./.stxt`, `~/.stxt` or the system level           |

		A CI example, which fails if any document does not validate or is not
		well formatted:

	Listing >>
		stxt validate --recursive docs/
		stxt format --check --recursive docs/

	Content >>
		The exit code distinguishes *your documents have errors* from *the command was
		misused*, so it can be used directly from a script. No command rewrites files
		without `--write`. Every option, output and exit code is in the
		[command-line reference](tools-cli).

	Subheader: Libraries

	Content >>
		Three implementations, one per ecosystem, with the same scope and the same version.
		All three derive from the specification through one shared reference pseudocode,
		and all three pass the same conformance corpus. None of them has runtime
		dependencies.

	Subsubheader: TypeScript / JavaScript

	Content >>
		[`@stxt-lang/core`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@stxt-lang/core) on npm. It is
		the parser the extension, the command line and the playground run on. The full
		guide —tree, validation, resolution, canonical JSON, writing— is in
		[the TypeScript library](tools-typescript).

	Listing >>
		npm install @stxt-lang/core

	Content >>
		A minimal example:

	TypeScript >>
		import { Parser, InlineNode } from '@stxt-lang/core';

		const result = new Parser().parseResult(text);
		for (const error of result.getErrors()) {
			console.error(`line ${error.line} [${error.code}]: ${error.message}`);
		}
		const root = result.getNodes()[0];
		if (root instanceof InlineNode) {
			console.log(root.getChild('Title')?.getText());
		}

	Subsubheader: Java

	Content >>
		[`dev.stxt:stxt-core`](https://central.sonatype.com/artifact/dev.stxt/stxt-core)
		on Maven Central. Java 17 or newer; automatic module `dev.stxt`. The full guide is
		in [the Java library](tools-java).

	Xml >>
		<dependency>
		    <groupId>dev.stxt</groupId>
		    <artifactId>stxt-core</artifactId>
		    <version>1.0.0</version>
		</dependency>

	Content >>
		Or with Gradle:

	Listing >>
		implementation 'dev.stxt:stxt-core:1.0.0'

	Subsubheader: Python

	Content >>
		[`stxt`](https://pypi.org/project/stxt/) on PyPI. Pure Python, 3.10 or newer. The full
		guide is in [the Python library](tools-python).

	Listing >>
		pip install stxt

	Content >>
		A minimal example:

	Python >>
		from stxt import Parser, InlineNode

		result = Parser().parse_result(text)
		for error in result.get_errors():
		    print(f"line {error.line} [{error.code}]: {error.message}")
		root = result.get_nodes()[0]
		if isinstance(root, InlineNode):
		    print(root.get_child("Title").get_text())

	Subheader: Source code

	Content >>
		The whole ecosystem lives in the
		[stxt-lang](https://github.com/stxt-lang) organization on GitHub, under the MIT
		license:

		| Repository                                                      | Contents                                              |
		|-----------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|
		| [stxt-web](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-web)               | This site: the specifications and the example corpus  |
		| [stxt-js](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-js)                 | `@stxt-lang/core`, the TypeScript library             |
		| [stxt-java](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-java)             | `dev.stxt:stxt-core`, the Java library                |
		| [stxt-python](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-python)         | `stxt`, the Python library                            |
		| [stxt-cli](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-cli)               | `@stxt-lang/cli`, the command line                    |
		| [stxt-vscode](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-vscode)         | The VS Code extension                                 |
		| [stxt-play](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-play)             | The playground                                        |

		Issues and proposals go to the repository of the tool concerned; those about the
		language itself, to `stxt-web`.
